<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Veronica Gambara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1485">1485</a>–<a href="/years/1550">1550</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Veronica Gambara was an Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> poet and stateswoman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>. She married Giberto X, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-da-correggio">Correggio</a>, in 1509 and after his death in 1518 governed the small state for her sons, defending it during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>. Her court attracted writers and artists, and she corresponded with Pietro Bembo, Pietro Aretino and others. Her sonnets and letters made her one of the best-known women poets of her age.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/veronica-gambara.jpg" alt="Veronica Gambara" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio da Correggio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Correggio,_Ritratto_di_dama,_c.1517-1518.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Veronica Gambara was born in Pralboino, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, one of the seven children of Count Gianfrancesco da Gambara and Alda Pio da Carpi. Her family included learned women: her great-aunts were the humanist poets Ginevre and Isotta Nogarola, and she was a niece of Emilia Pia, the principal female speaker of Castiglione's Il Cortegiano. She received a humanist education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Greek, philosophy, theology and scripture.</p><p>In 1502, at the age of 17, she began writing to Pietro Bembo, the leading neo-Petrarchan poet, who became her poetic mentor two years later when she started sending him her compositions. In 1509 she married her cousin, the count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-da-correggio">Correggio</a>, a widower of 50, at Amalfi. Their sons Ippolito and Girolamo were born in 1510 and 1511.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After her husband's death in 1518 she took charge of the state, including the management of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-da-correggio">Correggio</a>'s condottieri, and of the education of her two sons and her step-daughter Costanza. Under her rule the small court became something of a salon, visited by Gian Giorgio Trissino, Marcantonio Flaminio, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-ariosto">Ludovico Ariosto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a> as well as Bembo.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-da-correggio">Correggio</a> had been aligned with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a>, but she allied it with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. She received him at her estate in 1530, when he signed a treaty guaranteeing that Correggio would not again be besieged, and again in 1533. The treaty was broken in 1538 when Galeotto Pico II, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a> and Concordia, attacked the town; she organised a successful defence, and between 1546 and 1550 saw that Charles V paid for better fortifications. She died at Correggio on 13 June 1550.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>About 80 of her poems and 150 of her letters survive. Little of her verse was printed in her lifetime, but it circulated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> and was known throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by 1530. She wrote mainly in Italian, in four groups: political poems, devotional poems, Virgilian pastoral and love poems to her husband. The political poems express an idea of Italy as a single entity centuries before unification. Most are sonnets, with madrigals, ballads and stanze in ottava rima, and she also wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, including an ode greeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1530.</p><p>Besides Bembo she corresponded with Bernardo Tasso, Matteo Bandello and Pietro Aretino, who later slandered her as a laureated harlot, an attack she ignored. In a letter of 1549 to Ludovico Rosso she admits exhaustion with her duties and a wish to retire to a solitary life in the country. A complete English translation of her poems appeared in 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Veronica Gambara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Veronica Gambara</b>, poet and widow of Count Giberto, ruled as regent in the early 16th century and corresponded with leading writers.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-correggio">Principality of Correggio</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pralboino, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.27,10.22&amp;year=1527" aria-label="Pralboino on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4793 566 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Veronica Gambara"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5243 836h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5243 836h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.27,10.22&amp;year=1527">What was here in 1527? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
